Absolutely, it's not vanity at all.

278
00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.720
It's a severe life-threatening distortion of reality

279
00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:28.000
where the person feels literally trapped

280
00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:29.840
inside a monstrous version of themselves.

281
00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.200
That is heartbreaking.

282
00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:32.560
The distress is overwhelming

283
00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:35.040
because you cannot escape your own physical form.

284
00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:35.880
Right.

285
00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.440
If your trigger is a dirty doorknob,

286
00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:38.680
you can just walk away from the door.

287
00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:39.520
Right.

288
00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:40.560
If your trigger is your own face,

289
00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:41.760
there is no escape.

290
00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:42.880
You take it everywhere.

291
00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:44.720
And that leads to the second major

292
00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:46.560
clinical warning Keek notes.

293
00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:50.120
Cosmetic procedures do not fix body dysmorphic disorder.

294
00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:52.680
Okay, here's where it gets really interesting.

295
00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:55.360
Because the study guide explicitly states

296
00:09:55.360 --> 00:09:58.480
that if a patient with BDD undergoes plastic surgery

297
00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.880
to fix the perceived flaw,

298
00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:03.360
the preoccupation simply relocates.

299
00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:04.240
It just moves.

300
00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:06.720
It's like a nightmarish game of whack-a-mole.

301
00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:07.680
That's exactly what it is.

302
00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:11.400
Like someone agonizes over their nose for a decade.

303
00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:12.400
They get surgery.

304
00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:15.360
The nose is technically fixed by the surgeon,

305
00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.000
but within days that intense debilitating anxiety

306
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.480
just pops up on their chin or their ears or their jawline.

307
00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:24.600
Because you cannot use a surgical scalpel

308
00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:26.040
to treat a cognitive loop.

309
00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:28.120
Wow, put that on a T-shirt.

310
00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:29.240
Seriously though,

311
00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:31.120
the root issue is entirely

312
00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:33.240
in the brain's processing centers, right?

313
00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:36.080
It's not in the cartilage or the tissue of the body.

314
00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:38.960
Altering the physical tissue does absolutely nothing

315
00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:40.920
to rewire the neural pathways

316
00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:42.960
that are actively generating the obsession.

317
00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:44.200
That makes total sense.

318
00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.040
And before we leave BDD,

319
00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:48.880
Keek mentions a very specific diagnostic boundary

320
00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:50.240
that I think is really important.

321
00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:51.760
Of the eating disorder distinction.

322
00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:52.640
Yeah.

323
00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:55.160
The notes say if a patient's preoccupation

324
00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:58.520
is focused strictly on their overall weight and body shape,

325
00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:01.760
clinicians are instructed not to diagnose BDD.

326
00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:04.600
Right, because that shifts the diagnostic path entirely

327
00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:06.200
into the eating disorder category.

328
00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:07.920
Like anorexia or bulimia.

329
00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:08.960
Exactly.

330
00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.200
The clinician has to be incredibly precise

331
00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.880
about the exact nature of the perceived flaw

332
00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:15.360
because miscategorizing it

333
00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:18.080
could lead to the entirely wrong treatment protocol.

334
00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:18.920
Got it.

335
00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:20.160
Okay, let's follow that idea

336
00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.320
of externalizing the anxiety a bit further.

337
00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.600
We've gone from projecting onto the physical body

338
00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:26.400
to projecting onto physical objects.

339
00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:27.240
Ah, yes.

340
00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:28.960
Which brings us to hoarding disorder.

341
00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:29.800
Right.

342
00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:32.360
The core definition here is a persistent difficulty

343
00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:33.520
parting with possessions.

344
00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:36.120
And the critical phrase Keek emphasizes

345
00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:37.200
for clinicians to remember

346
00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:41.600
is completely regardless of their actual value.

347
00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:43.320
Regardless of actual value.

348
00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.360
So it's the brain assigning massive vital importance

349
00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:48.720
to things that are objectively valueless.

350
00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:49.560
Yes.

351
00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.600
We are talking about like stacks of old newspapers,

352
00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:56.480
broken electronics, junk mail flyers from five years ago.

353
00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:57.320
Exactly.

354
00:11:57.320 --> 00:11:58.600
And the mechanism driving this

355
00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:01.200
is a deeply felt need to save these items

356
00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.640
and severe acute distress

357
00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:05.440
at the mere thought of discarding them.

358
00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:07.480
And the clinical result is just devastating, right?

359
00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.560
The clutter completely fills and disables living spaces.

360
00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:11.440
It's tragic.

361
00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:13.560
The kitchen can no longer be used for cooking.

362
00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.640
The bathtub is filled to the ceiling with doxxas

363
00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:16.800
so they can't bathe.

364
00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.080
The bed can't be slept in.

365
00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:21.400
The possessions literally colonize the person's environment

366
00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.920
until the home just ceases to function as a home.

367
00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:24.840
That's a great way to put it.

368
00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:26.160
They colonize the space.

369
00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.440
Now Keek points out a major recent shift

370
00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:30.400
in the medical field regarding hoarding.

371
00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:32.280
Yes, a very important shift.

372
00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:34.840
It's now its own distinct diagnosis.

373
00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:37.040
It is no longer considered just a subset

374
00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.360
or like a flavor of OCD.

375
00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:41.480
But looking at the mechanics, I have a question.

376
00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:42.480
True.

377
00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:45.720
If someone is hoarding, say, broken toasters,

378
00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:49.600
how is their distress overthrowing one away

379
00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:52.800
different from a standard OCD compulsion?

380
00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:54.840
Aren't they just trying to prevent a disaster

381
00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:56.560
like in classic OCD?

382
00:12:56.560 --> 00:12:58.360
It's a really good distinction to make.

383
00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:01.560
The difference lies in where the value

384
00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.520
and the fear are actually anchored.

385
00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:04.560
Okay, what do you mean?

386
00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:07.880
Well, in classic OCD, the compulsion is performed

387
00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:12.080
to neutralize a specific terrifying intrusive thought

388
00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:14.840
that is often completely unrelated to the object itself.

389
00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:16.080
Okay, give me an example.

390
00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:19.200
So if I don't tap this doorknob three times,

391
00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.680
my family will die in a car crash.

392
00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:21.520
Oh, right.

393
00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:22.720
The doorknob has literally nothing to do

394
00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:23.560
with the car crash.

395
00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:25.280
It's just a magical thinking ritual.

396
00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:26.760
Precisely, it's magical thinking.

397
00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:30.080
But in hoarding, the distress is intrinsically tied

398
00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:31.480
to the physical object itself.

399
00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:34.080
The anxiety is about the loss of the item,

400
00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.520
the perceived waste of throwing it away,

401
00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:38.720
or, and this is a big one,

402
00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:42.880
the terrifying belief that throwing away an old receipt

403
00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:44.880
means permanently losing the memory

404
00:13:44.880 --> 00:13:46.160
associated with that day.

405
00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:47.000
Wow.

406
00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.000
The cognitive error is in how the brain

407
00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.000
calculates utility and emotional attachment.

408
00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:54.920
So they essentially anthropomorphize the objects.

409
00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:56.760
The junk mail isn't just paper.

410
00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:58.960
It feels like an extension of their own identity

411
00:13:58.960 --> 00:13:59.960
or their history.

412
00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:01.560
Which is why Keek notes that hoarding

413
00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:03.800
requires specialized CBT.

414
00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:05.920
Standard exposure and response prevention

415
00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:06.880
isn't sufficient here.

416
00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:08.680
Because it's not a magical thinking loop.

417
00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:10.760
Right, you can't just expose them to an empty room

418
00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:12.720
and expect habituation to happen.

419
00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:13.560
That wouldn't work?

420
00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:15.200
No, you have to actively rebuild

421
00:14:15.200 --> 00:14:16.760
their decision-making skills.

422
00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:19.240
You have to rebuild their categorization abilities

423
00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:22.640
and dismantle the very specific cognitive distortions

424
00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:24.840
they hold regarding the meaning of possessions.

425
00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:27.640
That reinforces the recurring theme perfectly.

426
00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:29.240
The one-size-fits-all treatment approach

427
00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:30.560
just utterly fails.

428
00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:31.400
It does?

429
00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:33.560
You really have to address the specific architecture

430
00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:34.680
of the delusion.

431
00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:36.760
Which brings us to the final two conditions

432
00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:38.680
Keek covers in this guide.

433
00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:41.480
The body-focused, repetitive behaviors.

434
00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.000
Right, we are moving entirely away

435
00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.760
from complex cognitive anxieties

436
00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:50.280
like agonizing over perceived facial flaws

437
00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:52.360
or saving junk mail to preserve a memory.

438
00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:53.880
You're leaving the cognitive realm.

439
00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:56.000
And entering the territory of pure,

440
00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:57.840
raw, physical habit loops.

441
00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:00.000
Yes, so we have trichotillomania,

442
00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.320
which is recurrent hair pulling

443
00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:03.640
leading to noticeable hair loss.

444
00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:05.520
And we have excordiation disorder,

445
00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:06.880
which is recurrent skin picking

446
00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.560
resulting in actual skin lesions.

447
00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.200
The detail in the notes that really stuck with me here

448
00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:16.920
is the phrase, with repeated attempts to stop.

449
00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:19.240
It is written right there in the diagnostic criteria

450
00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:20.760
for both of these conditions.

451
00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:22.480
And that detail alone, I mean,

452
00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:25.280
it reveals how frustrating and isolating this must be.

453
00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:26.120
It really does.

454
00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:27.760
The person is trying over and over again

455
00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:29.360
to stop pulling their eyebrows

456
00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:30.320
or picking their skin

457
00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:32.560
and they physically cannot stop themselves.

458
00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:33.800
It's vital to recognize

459
00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:37.160
that these are not deliberate acts of self-harm.

460
00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:38.960
That's a huge misconception.

461
00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:40.880
Right, they aren't doing it to hurt themselves.

462
00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:45.360
No, they're deeply ingrained, almost automatic urges.

463
00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:47.740
They often happen in this sort of trance-like state

464
00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:49.320
when the person is bored

465
00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:51.640
or reading a book or stressed.

466
00:15:51.640 --> 00:15:54.360
And because these are automatic physical habits

467
00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:56.600
rather than anxiety-driven rituals,

468
00:15:56.600 --> 00:15:59.520
we see the most radical shift in the treatment protocol.

469
00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:01.240
Yeah, the first line treatment here

470
00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:04.400
is habit reversal training or HRT.

471
00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:06.480
Which is a purely behavioral approach.

472
00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:08.740
Right, and KICK specifically underlines

473
00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:10.840
that this is not exposure therapy.

474
00:16:10.840 --> 00:16:12.080
No, definitely not.

475
00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:14.980
So if there's no fear, just a physical urge,

476
00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:16.680
does that mean you can't use ERP?

477
00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:18.640
I mean, you obviously can't expose someone

478
00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:21.480
to their own hair to trigger a fear response.

479
00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:24.280
So do you just have to physically block their hands

480
00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:26.040
from reaching their head?

481
00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:27.660
This raises an important question

482
00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:28.860
and it goes right to the heart

483
00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:30.800
of understanding these mechanisms.

484
00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.760
You aren't just blocking the hands,

485
00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:36.120
you are consciously rerouting the motor pathway.

486
00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:37.720
As we established earlier,

487
00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:40.400
exposure therapy is for confronting a fear.

488
00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:42.080
Right, the contamination fear.

489
00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:45.000
But with trichotillomania or excoriation,

490
00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:48.080
there is no terrifying intrusive thought.

491
00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:51.720
There is no malware pop-up warning of impending doom.

492
00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:52.720
Right, so what is it?

493
00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:54.240
It's just an itch in the brain.

494
00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:55.400
Basically, yes.

495
00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:58.120
It's a pure motor urge, a physical habit loop.

496
00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:01.000
So exposing them to the trigger does absolutely nothing.

497
00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:03.260
Okay, so how does habit reversal training

498
00:17:03.260 --> 00:17:04.640
actually work then?

499
00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:08.080
It involves bringing acute conscious awareness

500
00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:11.520
to a process that has become totally subconscious.

501
00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:14.720
The patient learns to recognize the exact micro-moment

502
00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:16.960
their hand begins to move toward their scalp

503
00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:18.000
or their arm.

504
00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:21.600
But okay, so once they catch that movement in real time.

505
00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.640
They are trained to immediately perform

506
00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:25.800
a physically incompatible response.

507
00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:27.520
A physically incompatible response.

508
00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:28.600
Yes, for example,

509
00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:30.600
the moment you feel the urge to pull your hair,

510
00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:32.820
you are trained to firmly clench your fists

511
00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:34.480
and press them down against your thighs.

512
00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:36.360
Or like grip the arms of your chair

513
00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:38.360
and just hold that incompatible posture

514
00:17:38.360 --> 00:17:39.900
until the urge naturally passes.

515
00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:41.840
Oh wow, so you are literally hacking

516
00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:43.160
the physical motor pathway.

517
00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:44.680
Because by clenching your fists,

518
00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:47.220
you physically cannot pull your hair.

519
00:17:47.260 --> 00:17:48.980
You replace the destructive habit

520
00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:50.580
with a neutral physical action.

521
00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:54.040
Exactly, you are rewriting the neurological habit loop

522
00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:55.900
through physical repetition,

523
00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:58.540
entirely bypassing the cognitive fear centers

524
00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:00.420
that ERP targets.

525
00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:01.620
So what does this all mean?

526
00:18:01.620 --> 00:18:03.420
Let's bring all these individual pieces together

527
00:18:03.420 --> 00:18:04.980
for the grand synthesis here.

528
00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:05.820
Okay.

529
00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:08.100
We've explored this entire family of disorders

530
00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:11.100
and we've seen how wildly the interventions must vary

531
00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.420
based on the specific nature of the loop.

532
00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:15.560
If we connect this to the bigger picture,

533
00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:17.560
you really have to praise the structure

534
00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:18.640
of this study material.

535
00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:20.200
Yeah, KICK did a great job.

536
00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:22.400
Because by rigorously categorizing

537
00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.420
the exact nature of the symptom,

538
00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:26.540
the solution just naturally reveals itself.

539
00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:27.380
Right.

540
00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:29.860
Classic OCD driven by intrusive fears

541
00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:32.760
and unwanted ego-distonic compulsions

542
00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:35.260
requires exposure and response prevention

543
00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:36.520
to break the fear cycle.

544
00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:39.500
Right, sitting with the anxiety until it habituates.

545
00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:42.160
Then we have body dysmorphic disorder

546
00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:45.480
where someone is trapped in a perceived external flaw

547
00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:46.880
that surgery cannot cure.

548
00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:48.360
Right, the whack-a-mole effect.

549
00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:51.000
And that gets CBT and SSRIs

550
00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:54.080
to address the cognitive distortion of self-image.

551
00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:55.840
Then there's hoarding disorder,

552
00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:58.720
rooted in an inability to discard items

553
00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:00.600
regardless of value.

554
00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:02.520
That requires specialized CBT

555
00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:04.840
focused on dismantling the emotional attachments

556
00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:08.200
